cell/did_kickoff	You go at that door like it owes you money. Boots, fists, and a range of opinions about everyone's mother. The cell next door cheers. The duty officer does not.
cell/did_listen	You put your ear to the cold steel and hold your breath. Two of them are talking at the desk, and they are not being careful.
cell/did_quiet	You sit with your back to the wall and your hands still and say nothing at all. The duty officer glances in twice, and the second time she nods.
cell/did_sleep	You lie back on the plastic and the ceiling stops moving eventually. Proper sleep, the kind you do not get on a bench.
cell/did_wall	You get talking to %1 through the breeze block, quietly, for a long time. Some things are easier said when you cannot see the other one's face.
cell/hazy_1	That went well, you think. That went rather well.
cell/hazy_2	You are fairly sure you handled that with some dignity.
cell/hazy_3	She seemed to take that in the spirit it was intended. Probably.
cell/hazy_4	Good. Yes. Nothing to worry about there.
cell/lost_krzysztof_1	You mean to sit quietly. What you actually do is apologise to the duty officer, at length, for the inconvenience. Then to the man next door. Then to the door.
cell/lost_krzysztof_2	You explain, carefully and repeatedly, that you do not really drink. You are lying on the floor at the time. She writes something down.
cell/lost_krzysztof_3	You spend twenty minutes trying to sort out the lad in the next cell's problems. He did not ask. He has more problems now.
cell/lost_marcin_1	You get onto the subject of Darek. Everyone in the building now knows about Darek. The duty officer knows what Darek does for a living.
cell/lost_marcin_2	You inform the officer, through the hatch, exactly what you think of him, his uniform, and the horse he probably wishes he had.
cell/lost_marcin_3	You cry for a bit, which is fine, and then you punch the wall, which is less fine. The wall wins on points.
cell/lost_sebastian_1	You attempt to do business with a police officer. Through a locked door. At two in the morning. You describe your inventory.
cell/lost_sebastian_2	You explain that you are only doing this until something better comes along, and have been for six years. Then you offer her a deal on something you do not have.
cell/lost_sebastian_3	You start listing your contacts by name to prove how well connected you are. She writes the names down. All of them.
cell/lost_tom_1	You recite most of a poem at the wall, in English, with feeling. Nobody here reads English. The wall is unmoved.
cell/lost_tom_2	You demand, in increasingly formal English, to speak to somebody about your passport. It is two in the morning and your passport has been gone for years.
cell/lost_tom_3	You deliver a short lecture on the English subjunctive to a man who is trying to sleep. He asks you to stop in two languages.
cell/morning_dried	You are dry, at least. Properly dry, for the first time in a while.
cell/morning_extra	They are not letting you out today. Nights they are keeping you on top: %1. You brought that on yourself and everybody knows it.
cell/morning_intro	Eight o'clock. The light is wrong and your mouth tastes of coins. Here is what you actually did last night.
cell/morning_item	%1
cell/morning_long	Several nights on that mattress have taken something out of you. You are stiff, you are hungry, and you smell like the inside of the van.
cell/morning_mended	Whatever got said through that wall, it held. You are on speaking terms again.
cell/morning_property	At the desk they slide your things back across in a bag. You get %1 percent of what they took.
cell/morning_shakes	You come out of there wanting a drink more than you have wanted anything in a long time. Your hands are not steady and the light is too bright. Whatever drying out they did for you, they did it the hard way.
