gather/result_2	A patient rummage yields %1 bottles and something sticky you decide not to identify.
gather/result_3	%1 bottles, one of them still half full. You pour it out. Standards.
gather/result_full	You grab %1 more, but the trolley is full. Time to cash them in.
give/already	You have already sorted %1 out today. Do not overdo it.
give/hate	%1 looks at it, then at you, then hands it back. That was a misjudgement.
give/like	%1 takes it with a proper nod. That went down well.
give/love	%1 lights up. That was exactly the right thing and they will not forget it.
give/nothing	You pat your pockets. You have absolutely nothing worth giving anyone.
give/ok	%1 accepts it politely enough. It is the thought, apparently.
give/title	What do you give them?
howto/about_1	A żul, and you say it zhool, is what the Poles call a man who has made the pavement his own. He has a bench. He has a bottle. It is ten in the morning and he is doing fine, thank you for asking.
howto/about_2	That is you. Congratulations.
howto/about_3	You have a shopping trolley, a coat that has seen the inside of a bin, and a whole district that clocked you years ago and drew its conclusions. There is no winning this. There is only Tuesday, and then another Tuesday.
howto/about_4	What you do is bins. You do bins until the trolley is full, you take the bottles to the machine that gives you coins for them, and then you decide, like a grown man with choices, whether that money is food or whether it is not food. It is usually not food.
howto/about_5	Do this long enough and things stick. People start expecting you. The woman in the shop puts something aside without being asked. A copper learns your face, which cuts both ways. Somebody remembers your name, or makes a point of forgetting it, and that is on you.
howto/about_6	Everyone in this town has a name and a temper and something they will not shut up about. Be decent to them and it comes back. Be a prick and that comes back faster.
howto/about_7	Fair warning: the language in here would strip paint.
howto/basics_1	Arrow keys move you. Hold a direction and you keep walking. The ground tells you where you are: grass, gravel, mud and pavement all sound different under your boots, and you will learn them faster than you expect.
howto/basics_2	Enter is the whole game, really. It uses whatever you are standing on, talks to whoever you have just walked into, and gets you through a conversation. If you are not sure what to do, stand on something and press Enter.
howto/basics_3	L looks around and tells you where you are and what is near. Use it constantly. There is no shame in it and it is quicker than wandering.
howto/basics_4	The clock runs on its own whether you are doing anything or not, so a day slips by while you are stood there thinking. Places keep hours. The bar is shut in the morning and the shop is not open all night.
howto/basics_5	Nothing here kills you and nothing here ends. A bad day is just a bad day, and tomorrow the bins will be full again.
howto/body_1	You run on energy, out of a hundred, and it is the real limit on your day. Rummaging, washing windscreens, shifting crates, begging, brawling: all of it costs some. Press E to hear how you are holding up.
howto/body_2	Nothing comes back on its own. Standing about waiting will not put an hour of your life back, so what you have is what you have until you eat, drink or sleep. Go to bed with nothing left and you get less back from the night than a man who paced himself.
howto/body_3	Hunger climbs on its own and drags at you, and eating is how you get your legs back, so food is not a treat, it is the job. A roll is two złoty and a bit of energy. A kebab is twenty and most of an afternoon. Go to bed hungry and the night gives you less.
howto/body_4	Health is separate and mostly only moves when somebody hits you or the night does. A night indoors puts it right.
howto/body_5	Sitting on a bench, Enter on it, gets a bit of breath back and tells you who is about. It will pull you up off the floor but no further: a bench is not a bed and it is not a meal. It costs you twenty minutes of daylight instead of money, which some days is the better bargain.
howto/body_6	So if you are on your knees at two in the afternoon, standing still until four will not help you. Something to eat, a hot drink, a sit down, or the night. Those are the four.
howto/body_7	Food and a hot drink put energy back on the spot. Coffee and tea pick you up. The strong stuff does the opposite, whatever it feels like at the time.
howto/drink_1	Drink is the whole trouble and half the point. It makes the day easier to be inside of and it makes you worse at everything in it.
howto/drink_2	Being drunk makes you feel fresher than you are, which is a lie your body tells you and the game tells you too. Past a certain point you stagger, you trip, and the world starts to swim about.
howto/drink_3	Do it often enough and it stops being a choice. A habit builds, and when it is not fed you get the shakes: everything costs more and you are worse at all of it until you have had one.
howto/drink_4	It does wear off, but slowly, about two hours of the day to come down a step. If you want it gone faster, eat something, get a tea or a coffee down you, or sit on a bench a while. That is what the food and the hot drinks are for.
howto/drink_5	You can turn the drunk sound effects off in Options if the swimming gets unpleasant. Nobody will think less of you.
howto/first_1	Stop reading this. Go and be poor for a bit and come back if it goes badly. Still here? Fine.
howto/first_2	You are on ulica Wesoła, which means Cheerful Street, and somebody in the town planning office was having a laugh. Arrow keys walk you about. Hold one down to keep going. Press L and it tells you where you are and what is near, like a barman who has seen you come in.
howto/first_3	Find the bins. Walk east along the street until you hear them, stand on them, and press Enter. Press Enter again. And again. This is the job. Nobody said it was a good job. Each go puts bottles in your trolley, and the trolley only holds so many.
howto/first_4	Trolley full? The bottle deposit is on the same street. Stand on it, press Enter, and it turns your rubbish into actual money at one złoty a bottle. Enjoy the moment. It is the most reliable money you will ever make.
howto/first_5	Now eat something before your legs go. The shop is on the main street: stand on it, press Enter, and it opens like any other place. Food fills you up and puts a bit of yourself back. This is not advice, it is physics.
howto/first_6	Somebody will walk into you, because they always do. You will hear the bump and who it was. Press Enter and have a word with them. It costs nothing, it makes them think better of you, and being liked round here is worth more than the coins in your pocket.
howto/first_7	Press Q whenever you like and it will tell you what the day expects of you. Three jobs, every morning, and finishing one pays on the spot. The day expects a lot, considering.
howto/first_8	Watch your energy, which is the E key. Everything worth doing costs some, and when it is gone there is no more work in you until you get something inside you or sit down a while. Nothing comes back just from waiting.
howto/first_9	When you are running on empty, get back to your block on the main street and press Enter to turn in. It will ask whether you mean it. Sleeping outside is a choice available to you, and so is regret.
howto/keys_1	Arrows move. Enter uses what is under you, talks to who you walked into, and advances dialogue. Any arrow repeats the current dialogue line.
howto/keys_2	L looks. I opens your bag, and Enter in there eats or drinks whatever you are on. P is the people menu. Q is your daily quests. S reads your full status. V says something in character.
howto/keys_3	C, R and H are quick checks: cash, respect, health. T picks a place to track and starts a beeper toward it, W reads the direction in words.
howto/keys_4	Page Up and Page Down set the music volume, and Page Down mutes it at the bottom. Home and End set the sound effects volume, in the menus as well as while you are walking about. Inside a list, they jump you to the top and the bottom of it instead.
howto/keys_5	F5 saves in a hurry, wherever you are. Shift and B opens the builder, if you fancy making an area of your own.
howto/keys_6	E tells you how you are holding up in one line, where S gives you the lot.
howto/keys_7	Escape pauses the game: carry on, save, Options, or quit. Options holds the drunk sound effects, the auto-save, the language, whether you are modding the game, and the tool for putting damaged files back. It is on the main menu too.
howto/law_1	The police notice things. Fighting in the street, being blind drunk where people can see you, singing at the sky, giving a copper lip: all of it builds their interest in you.
howto/law_2	Where and when matters. The crossroads, the station, and the ground outside the police station itself are all watched, that last one most of all. The small hours are worse than the afternoon, because there are fewer people to be lost among.
howto/law_3	At low interest a copper will just move you on. Once you are of real interest to them it becomes a proper stop: searched, asked your name, and asked things about other people.
howto/law_4	You can pay the fine or refuse to sign the ticket. Refusing costs you nothing today and a great deal of their attention afterwards.
howto/law_5	They will ask you to give somebody up. You can. It gets you an easier time from them for a long while afterwards, and the street finds out, because the street always finds out.
howto/law_6	Get nicked and you lose what is in your pocket and you spend the night inside, which is four turns you actually play. You can sleep it off, listen at the door, talk through the wall to whoever is next door, kick off, or sit there quietly. Sitting quietly gets you more of your things back in the morning. Kicking off gets you an extra night and a bit of a reputation.
howto/law_7	If you are drunk when they take you, you will find you do not always do the thing you chose. You meant to sit quietly and instead you are explaining yourself to a locked door. What comes out depends on who you are, and while you are in there you will think it went rather well. The morning tells you otherwise.
howto/law_8	Somebody you know will end up in the cells. Go and see them. It costs you an afternoon and it is worth more to them than you would think.
howto/lines_1	You can fight people. Press the option when you have walked into somebody and it will ask if you are sure, because it should.
howto/lines_2	A fight is rounds: swing, throw everything at it, cover up, or leg it. Covering up halves what lands. Legging it costs you a bit of face and ends it.
howto/lines_3	Winning builds street respect. Losing does the opposite, and either way the police take an interest, and whoever you hit will remember it for a long time.
howto/lines_4	There is one thing this game will not let you do lightly, and it will ask you twice. Do it and the whole town closes against you at once, and you will live marked for days. It is in here because a game about this life would be dishonest without it.
howto/money_1	Bottles are the bread and butter. Stand on the bins, press Enter, and rummage. Your trolley fills up, and the deposit machine on the main street turns them into coins at one złoty each.
howto/money_2	A bigger trolley holds more, and the garage at the market sells them. Your eye for a good bin also sharpens the more you do it, so the hundredth rummage pays better than the first.
howto/money_3	Windscreens at the crossroads pay better than bins, and pay better again if the district thinks well of you. Odd jobs shifting crates at the market pay too, and cost you more of yourself.
howto/money_4	Begging works, some of the time, and depends entirely on who you ask and how they feel about you. Ask the same person twice in a day and see how far it gets you.
howto/money_5	The shop sells what comes in a packet or a bottle. The stand on the market does hot food and a hot drink, and the man behind it will talk to you if you let him.
howto/money_6	Things turn up on the ground. Some of it is rubbish, some of it is somebody's favourite thing in the world, and giving that to the right person is worth more than selling it.
howto/money_7	Money is not the point but you will not get far without it. Nothing here is expensive. Everything here is expensive when you have nine złoty.
howto/people_1	Fifteen people live round here and you will come to know all of them: the old boy, the babcia, the schemer, the drunk, the shop kid, the commuter, the rival żul, the copper, the barman, the kiosk woman, the mother, the sister, the schoolgirl, the duty officer at the station, and the man on the food stand.
howto/people_2	Everybody gives off a quiet murmur you can home in on, and everybody's is different. Walk into somebody and you hear the bump and who it was. Press P for a list of who is about and how far off they are.
howto/people_3	Talk to people. Press Enter when you have bumped into them, or use the P menu. It costs you almost nothing and it is how every good thing in this game starts.
howto/people_4	How you stand with somebody builds over days, not minutes. Keep turning up and it grows. Ignore them for a week and it cools. Talk to the same person six times in one afternoon and they will start to wonder about you.
howto/people_5	Everybody has one thing they love off the shelf and one thing they love that you can only find lying about. Get it right and they will not forget it. Get it wrong and they will let you know.
howto/people_6	They give things back. Somebody who thinks well of you will put something in your hand for nothing, and it is usually the thing you needed.
howto/people_7	The district as a whole has an opinion of you too, separate from any one person. Being decent in public builds it. Brawling in the street does the opposite.
howto/people_8	There is also street respect, which is a different currency entirely. That comes from being someone nobody fancies testing, and the two do not always pull the same way.
howto/people_9	The sister runs a soup round. If you are broke and starving she will feed you and ask for nothing, and no, there is no catch.
howto/pick	How to play. Pick a section, Escape to go back.
howto/quests_1	Every morning brings three jobs, one of each sort: something to graft at, something involving people, and something that is just living your life. Press Q to hear them.
howto/quests_2	Each one tells you what it wants and what it pays. Finish one and you are paid on the spot.
howto/quests_3	Behind those sit nine milestones, the longer road. They are earned once and they stay earned: a real friend on the street, a face the whole district knows, a night in the cells you came out the other side of.
howto/quests_4	A locked milestone will not tell you the steps, only what to chase. Where there is something worth counting, it says how far along you are.
howto/quests_5	The quest panel also keeps your record: how many day jobs you have finished, and how many milestones you have unlocked out of the nine.
