mod/fix_it_now	Look at the files and put back whatever I choose
mod/its_your_game	If it was deliberate, carry on. It is your copy and swapping the sounds about is a perfectly good thing to do with it.
mod/keep_asking	Yes, tell me again next time.
mod/noticed	Something is different about this copy of the game. %1 files are missing and %2 have been changed.
mod/stop_asking	No, I am changing things on purpose. Stop mentioning it.
mod/what_it_means	That is not a problem in itself, but it does mean the game might go quiet where it should not, or behave oddly, and it will not be a fault in the game itself.
mod/what_now	What would you like to do?
ms/cellvisit_how	Somebody you know will end up inside. Go and see them.
ms/cellvisit_name	Nobody visits, but you did
ms/earned	Milestone reached: %1.
ms/firstarrest_how	Sooner or later they will have you. Come out the other side.
ms/firstarrest_name	A night in the cells
ms/firstfriend_how	Keep turning up for somebody until they count you a friend.
ms/firstfriend_name	A friend on the street
ms/hardman_how	Build the sort of reputation nobody fancies testing.
ms/hardman_name	Not to be messed with
ms/locked	(not yet)
ms/reward_cash	That is worth %1 złoty to you.
ms/reward_community	The street thinks a little more of you. Community up %1.
ms/reward_heat	The heat comes off you a little. The law has bigger worries tonight.
ms/reward_scrounge	Your eye for a good bin is sharper now. You will find more from here on.
ms/reward_street	Word gets round. Street respect up %1.
ms/scavenger_how	Turn in bottles. Then turn in a great many more.
ms/scavenger_name	King of the bins
ms/souprun_how	There is a sister who feeds people. Be there when you have nothing.
ms/souprun_name	Fed when it mattered
ms/survived_how	Cross the line and then live through what the street does about it.
ms/survived_name	You lived it down
ms/tickettorn_how	Somewhere there is a copper with a book out. Do not sign it.
ms/tickettorn_name	I am not signing that
ms/wellknown_how	Get the whole street knowing your face, not just the one person.
ms/wellknown_name	A known face
npc/babcia_blunt	You tell her you're grand and to mind her own. She sniffs, calls you a bezczelny gnojek under her breath, crosses herself for saying it, and wheels off. You have made an enemy of a woman with connections.
npc/babcia_desc	A small determined woman with a shopping trolley, a headscarf, and the moral authority of a bishop.
npc/babcia_left	You mumble something and shuffle away before the sandwich negotiation can begin.
npc/babcia_line1	She stops in front of you, looks you up and down, and asks when you last ate something hot.
npc/babcia_line2	Without waiting for an answer she starts telling you about her grandson, who is also thin, and who never calls.
npc/babcia_short	The grandmother with the trolley
npc/babcia_warm	You ask after the grandson by name. She lights up, presses a wrapped sandwich into your hand and tells you to eat it before it goes cold.
npc/barman_blunt	You tell him to mind his own bar. He goes very still, dries a glass, says nothing. The nothing is worse than anything he could say.
npc/barman_desc	A broad, unhurried man who has stood behind this bar for twenty years and has heard every story in the district at least twice.
npc/barman_left	You leave it there. He goes back to the glass he was drying.
npc/barman_line1	He looks up, works out roughly how your day has gone from the state of you, and says nothing about it.
npc/barman_line2	He mentions, without accusing anybody, that somebody has been leaving empties on the windowsill outside.
npc/barman_short	The man behind the bar
npc/barman_warm	You tell him it wasn't you, and that you'd tell him if it was. He decides to believe you, which is worth more than the beer.
npc/budkarz_blunt	You tell him it is just a sausage. He goes quiet, wounded to his core, and charges you full whack from now on.
npc/budkarz_desc	He has run the same hot-food stand at the edge of the market for years, feeds half the district after closing time, and is fiercely, genuinely proud of his sausage.
npc/budkarz_left	He is already serving the next person before you have turned around.
npc/budkarz_line1	He is turning sausages with a pair of tongs and does not stop while he talks to you.
npc/budkarz_line2	He tells you the secret is the onions, then immediately says he will not tell you the secret.
npc/budkarz_short	The man on the food stand
npc/budkarz_warm	You tell him it is the best food in town, and you mean it. He beams, and slips an extra bit onto your portion when he thinks you are not looking.
npc/bump_1	You walk straight into %1 and both of you apologise at the same time.
npc/bump_2	You bump shoulders with %1. Neither of you was watching where you were going.
npc/bump_3	You blunder into %1, who steps aside with the patience of someone used to it.
npc/dojazd_blunt	You ask him what he's staring at. He wasn't staring, and now he's frightened, and now you feel worse.
npc/dojazd_desc	A man in a work jacket doing everything in his power to avoid eye contact with you.
npc/dojazd_left	You leave him to his imaginary phone call.
npc/dojazd_line1	He is checking his phone with the intensity of a man who has nothing to check.
npc/dojazd_line2	He very nearly says something, then thinks better of it and studies the timetable instead.
npc/dojazd_short	The commuter
npc/dojazd_warm	You say good morning like a normal person. He is so surprised he says it back, and looks at you properly for the first time.
npc/dyzurna_blunt	You tell her this is ridiculous. She agrees, entirely, and does nothing whatsoever about it.
npc/dyzurna_desc	She sits behind a sliding glass panel with a stamp, a form, and a monumental indifference to whatever has happened to you. She is not unkind. You are simply not her problem.
npc/dyzurna_left	You leave the counter. The glass slides shut before you have turned round.
npc/dyzurna_line1	She slides the glass across about four inches and waits without any expression at all.
npc/dyzurna_line2	She says the form you want is not the form you have, and this appears to be the end of the matter.
npc/dyzurna_short	The woman on the duty desk
npc/dyzurna_warm	You fill in the right form, in the right box, in capitals. Something almost like approval crosses her face and is gone.
npc/gives_1	%1 digs about, comes up with %2, and presses it on you before you can argue.
npc/gives_2	Without making anything of it, %1 hands you %2. That is what mates are for.
npc/gives_3	%1 says they have got a spare, which is a lie, and gives you %2 anyway.
npc/kioskarka_blunt	You tell her to lighten up. The hatch does not close, but it might as well have.
npc/kioskarka_desc	She has run the kiosk on the corner for eleven years, sells papers, tickets and cigarettes through a hatch, and has heard every excuse in the district delivered through that same small window.
npc/kioskarka_left	She goes back to the crossword before you have finished turning.
npc/kioskarka_line1	She is doing the crossword and does not look up straight away, which is not rudeness, just how she is.
npc/kioskarka_line2	She says somebody tried to pay her in coppers this morning and she is still annoyed about it.
npc/kioskarka_short	The woman on the kiosk
npc/kioskarka_warm	You agree that coppers are an insult and she decides you are alright.
npc/kombinator_blunt	You tell him his last three schemes ended with somebody in a cell. He takes it well, because it's true, calls himself a visionary ahead of his time, and respects you a bit more for saying it.
npc/kombinator_desc	A wiry bloke in a tracksuit who is always mid-scheme and always needs one more person for it to work.
npc/kombinator_left	You walk off mid-pitch. He carries on pitching to a bin.
npc/kombinator_line1	He appears at your elbow, glances both ways, and says he has something going. He always has something going.
npc/kombinator_line2	It involves a van, a mate of his cousin, and a quantity of something he describes only as 'stock'. He needs a hand and swears it's clean.
npc/kombinator_short	The lad with the scheme
npc/kombinator_warm	You tell him to count you in if it ever actually happens. He grins, calls you a legend, and swears on his mother's grave it's foolproof. His mother is alive. It will never happen.
npc/krawezniak_blunt	You point out you've done nothing and ask if he's got real criminals to chase. His jaw tightens. That'll cost you next time, and with him there is always a next time.
npc/krawezniak_desc	A local officer who has moved you along so many times it has become almost a working relationship.
npc/krawezniak_left	You move along before he can finish the sentence. He watches you the whole way.
npc/krawezniak_line1	He stops a few feet away, hands behind his back, and asks how you're getting on. It is not entirely a threat.
npc/krawezniak_line2	He mentions there have been complaints. He does not say from who, and he does not say he believes them.
npc/krawezniak_short	The copper who knows your face
npc/krawezniak_warm	You're polite, you don't argue, you tell him you'll keep out of the way. He nods once and lets it go. Filed under 'no trouble', which is the best a man like you can hope for.
npc/matka_blunt	You speak too loudly and too close. She walks on and does not look back.
npc/matka_desc	She is pushing a pram round the courtyard on almost no sleep, and has exactly no time for anybody's nonsense today, or any other day.
npc/matka_left	You leave her to it. She was already going.
npc/matka_line1	She keeps the pram between you and her without making anything of it.
npc/matka_line2	She mentions the little one was up half the night, in the flat tone of somebody who has stopped expecting sympathy.
npc/matka_short	The young mother with the pram
npc/matka_warm	You keep your voice down and step aside for the pram. She notices, and says so, which costs her something.
npc/not_here_now	You look round for them and they are not here any more. Whatever that was, the moment has gone.
npc/pijak_blunt	You tell him he's talking nonsense. He agrees enthusiastically, says nonsense is underrated, and carries on anyway.
npc/pijak_desc	A man considerably further gone than you, holding a bottle and an idea he desperately needs to share.
npc/pijak_left	You gently unhook your sleeve and go. He does not notice.
npc/pijak_line1	He grabs your sleeve with great urgency and begins explaining something enormously important.
npc/pijak_line2	As far as you can tell it involves the government, his brother-in-law, and a dog he once had. The three may be connected. He seems certain.
npc/pijak_short	The proper drunk
npc/pijak_warm	You nod along and tell him he's absolutely right. He wells up, calls you the only kurwa honest man left, and offers you a swig you politely decline.
npc/reply_ask	Ask them for something
npc/reply_blunt	Give it to them straight
npc/reply_fight	Square up to them
npc/reply_give	Give them something
npc/reply_leave	Make your excuses and go
npc/reply_warm	Be decent about it
npc/siostra_blunt	You tell her you do not need saving. She agrees with you, pleasantly, and that is somehow worse.
npc/siostra_desc	She does the soup run three nights a week, feeds anybody who turns up, and has never once lectured a soul about how they got there.
npc/siostra_left	You leave. She does not try to stop you, which is why people come back.
npc/siostra_line1	She asks if you have eaten today, and waits for the real answer rather than the polite one.
npc/siostra_line2	She tells you there is soup on Thursday and says it like a fact, not a favour.
npc/siostra_short	The sister from the parish
npc/siostra_warm	You tell her the truth about how the week has gone. She takes it without a flicker and tells you Thursday, again.
npc/sklepowy_blunt	You tell him he doesn't know he's born. He shrugs and goes back inside early. Probably fair.
npc/sklepowy_desc	A bored teenager from the corner shop, out the back on a five minute break that has been going twenty minutes.
npc/sklepowy_left	You leave him to his break. He looks almost disappointed.
npc/sklepowy_line1	He nods at you the way you nod at someone you see every day and know nothing about.
npc/sklepowy_line2	He complains about his manager, his hours, and a customer who counted out payment in one groszy coins.
npc/sklepowy_short	The shop kid on a fag break
npc/sklepowy_warm	You tell him the coin story is the funniest thing you've heard all week. He laughs properly and says you're alright. Small mercies at the till later.
npc/stary_blunt	You tell him his day is over and the works are a car park now. He goes quiet. You feel about six inches tall, but the lads would have laughed.
npc/stary_desc	An old boy in a flat cap who has lived on this estate since before the blocks went up, and has opinions about all of it.
npc/stary_left	You make a noise about being somewhere and go. He carries on talking to the space you were in.
npc/stary_line1	He shuffles up, taps the bench beside him and tells you the estate has gone downhill since they closed the works.
npc/stary_line2	He explains, at length, that in his day a man could get a job and a flat by simply asking. He is not entirely wrong and not entirely right.
npc/stary_short	The old boy on the bench
npc/stary_warm	You let him talk himself out and agree in all the right places. He pats your arm and says you're a good lad, which costs him something to say.
npc/talk_hint	Press Enter to talk to them, or walk on.
npc/uczennica_blunt	You tell her to get on with it. She calls you a boomer, which stings more than any swear word, and the earphone goes straight back in.
npc/uczennica_desc	Sixteen, headphones round her neck, bag over one shoulder, taking the short cut through the estate twice a day and clocking everything.
npc/uczennica_left	She is already walking. The music goes back on.
npc/uczennica_line1	She has one earphone out, which is her being polite.
npc/uczennica_line2	She says the bus was cancelled again and the whole class was late, and she wants you to agree it is ridiculous.
npc/uczennica_short	The schoolgirl cutting through
npc/uczennica_warm	You agree the buses are absolutely kurwa hopeless. She cackles, delighted an adult finally said it, and tells you the driver's a legend for not caring either.
npc/zul_obcy_blunt	You tell him the spot behind the shop was yours first. He tells you to spierdalaj. Neither of you moves. Something has been established, and it isn't friendship.
npc/zul_obcy_desc	A żul from the bench across the way, sizing you up to work out whether you're competition or company.
npc/zul_obcy_left	You give him nothing and walk. He'll remember that.
npc/zul_obcy_line1	He asks, without much warmth, how the bins have been treating you lately.
npc/zul_obcy_line2	He mentions that his lot have got the good spot behind the shop now, and watches your face carefully when he says it.
npc/zul_obcy_short	The żul from the other bench
npc/zul_obcy_warm	You say fair play and wish him luck with it. He relaxes, calls you alright for a cwaniak, and tells you about a skip nobody's touched yet.
oddjob/result_1	You shift crates until your back complains, and a trader presses %1 złoty into your hand.
oddjob/result_2	Half an hour of hauling boxes nobody else wanted to haul. Worth %1 złoty and a nod of respect.
oddjob/result_3	You help a stallholder pack down, break nothing, and get %1 złoty for your trouble.
