police/arrested_line	They do not talk to you on the way. %1 złoty goes into an envelope. Nobody offers you anything.
police/arrested_rough	They put you in the van. %1 złoty out of your pocket goes into an envelope with your name on it.
police/asks_about_1	He asks who has been taking the copper wire off the substation. He already half knows. He wants you to say it.
police/asks_about_2	He asks who was drinking in the courtyard last night, and you know full well who it was.
police/confiscated	He takes %1 items off you and puts them in a bag. You will not see them again.
police/cop_stops_1	A patrol car slows to walking pace beside you and the window comes down.
police/cop_stops_2	Policja. He asks for your name and does not write it down, which is worse.
police/cop_stops_3	He stops you with one hand and does not take it off your shoulder while he talks.
police/grass	Give him the name
police/grassed	You give him the name. He writes it down properly this time. Something goes out of the afternoon, and it does not come back.
police/guard_stops_1	Two city guards peel off the wall they were holding up. One of them says your name is not the point, the point is what you are doing on that bench.
police/guard_stops_2	A city guard steps in front of you with the weary air of a man three hours from the end of his shift.
police/guard_stops_3	City guard. He looks at the bottle, then at you, then back at the bottle, and waits for you to say something clever.
police/how_do_you_play_it	How do you play this?
police/known_now	That is not the first time, and this is a small district. People will know by Thursday. They always know by Thursday.
police/let_off_1	He decides you are not worth the paperwork and tells you to move along.
police/let_off_2	He looks at you for a long moment, then waves you off. Not today.
police/mandat	He starts writing a mandat karny. %1 złoty.
police/mandat_choice	Do you take the ticket?
police/paid	You pay. He tears off the slip and hands it over almost politely, and that is that.
police/pay_it	Take it and pay
police/refuse_it	Refuse to accept it
police/refused	You refuse to sign. Now it goes to a court, and now it is paperwork for him, which he enjoys as much as you do. The street will hear you did not sign.
police/reply_0_said	You are politeness itself. Yes sir, no sir, absolutely sir. It costs you nothing and he visibly relaxes.
police/reply_1_said	You answer what he asks and not one word more. He respects it, or at least he cannot fault it.
police/reply_2_said	You give him just enough lip to enjoy yourself. His jaw sets.
police/reply_3_said	You tell him precisely what you think of him, his shift, and his uniform. The temperature drops about ten degrees.
police/reply_cheeky	Give him a bit of cheek
police/reply_polite	Yes officer, sorry officer, whatever you say
police/reply_rude	Tell him exactly where to go
police/reply_straight	Answer him straight and say nothing extra
police/said_nothing	You tell him you did not see anything. He knows you are lying. You know he knows. Nobody says so.
police/say_nothing	Say nothing
police/slipped_krzysztof_1	You mean to be polite. It comes out as a long, swaying apology for things you have not done, finishing with the information that you do not really drink. He looks at the bottle in your hand.
police/slipped_krzysztof_2	You mean to keep it simple. Instead you try to shake his hand, twice, and ask after his mother by a name you have invented.
police/slipped_marcin_1	You mean to be civil. What arrives is Darek. Who Darek is, what Darek did, and what you would do to Darek given ten minutes and a quiet street.
police/slipped_marcin_2	You mean to answer the question. You get three words in before your voice goes and you are jabbing a finger at his chest, which is a mistake, and you know it while you are doing it.
police/slipped_sebastian_1	You mean to be straight with him. Somehow you end up explaining what you could do for him on price, if he was ever after anything, which he was not, and now he is interested for the wrong reason.
police/slipped_sebastian_2	You mean to keep it short. You tell him you are only doing this until something better comes up. He asks doing what. That is a very good question and you have just asked it of yourself.
police/slipped_tom_1	You mean to be polite, and you are, extremely, in English, at length, with subordinate clauses. He understands one word in nine and none of them help.
police/slipped_tom_2	You mean to answer him. You correct his grammar instead. It was not even wrong. You are not sure why you did that.
police/tank	The sobering station. They take your laces, they take your belt, and in the morning they take %1 złoty for the privilege of the bed. You are sober, which is the point, and poorer, which is also the point.
police/what_do_you_say	What do you say?
