howto/build_1	You can build areas of your own and they become part of the game. Shift and B opens the builder from anywhere, and it is worth knowing that before you need it.
howto/build_2	An area is a patch of ground with things in it. It has a size, a way in, something underfoot, and then whatever you put there. Everything you build is saved as you go, into a plain text file, and you never have to open that file yourself.
howto/build_3	You build by walking. Stand where you want a bench and tell it to put a bench there. It takes the spot from where you are standing, so there are no numbers to work out, and it will let you type them instead if you would rather.
howto/build_4	There are six things you can add. A place is something you press Enter on: a bench, a bin worth going through, a shop, somewhere to bed down. A person is somebody who stands there and can be talked to. A description area is scenery you hear when you walk through it. A ground surface changes how your footsteps sound. A sound source is something making a noise in a fixed spot. And an exit is a way out to somewhere else.
howto/build_5	Putting somebody on a map asks who, from everybody in the game, and when they are about. All day is the plain answer, or you can give them hours so they come and go, which is what makes a town feel lived in rather than staged. Putting the same person down twice moves them rather than leaving two of them about.
howto/build_6	When you put down a shop, a bar or a food stand it asks who is behind the counter. Anybody in the game will do, or nobody in particular, which leaves whoever the game normally uses. If you name somebody, it also asks whether the place should be shut when they are not about, which is how you make a shop that closes at ten and a town you have to plan your evening around.
howto/build_7	For anything covering a stretch of ground, a description area or a patch of surface, you walk to one corner and press Enter, then walk to the other. Or you type the four edges, if you already know where they are.
howto/sec_translate	Translating the game
howto/translate_1	The game can speak any language somebody is willing to write, and there is a tool in it to do the writing. It is under Options, called Translation, and it is also in the builder.
howto/translate_2	It shows you every line the game can say, one after another. For each one you hear the English and either what you have already put or that nobody has done it yet, with a count of how many are finished. Press Enter on a line to type your version.
howto/translate_3	There is an option to jump straight to the next line nobody has touched, which is how you would actually sit down and work through it rather than arrowing past a thousand lines that are already done.
howto/translate_4	You can search it too. It looks in the English, in what you have written yourself, and in the name of the line, so you can find how you put something before and keep it consistent. It ignores accents, so you do not need the right keyboard to find your own work.
howto/translate_5	It will translate a single area as well as the game, so somebody can add a language to one place without touching anything else. And it works whichever way round: an area written in Polish can have English added to it just as easily.
howto/translate_6	Nothing is the original. English and Polish sit beside each other as equals, and anything you start does too. If a line has not been done in your language you hear the English rather than silence, so a half finished translation is still perfectly playable.
howto/translate_7	To start a language nobody has done, choose Start a new language and give it a plain one word name like german. Everything you save goes into a file of that name, and the language then turns up in the options like any other.
