char/halina_name	Halina
char/halina_intro	Sixty one years old, thirty of them out here. I know who owes what to who, and I know what you had for breakfast. Sit down, you look terrible.
char/halina_quirk	Been on this street longer than the shop has. Trades in what she knows rather than what she has, which is nothing. Makes her own nalewka and has strong views on what other people call nalewka. Checks on people without ever admitting to it.
char/halina_story1	Halina had a flat on the fourth floor, a husband, and a job at the cannery, in roughly that order of importance to her.
char/halina_story2	The cannery went first. Then the husband went, which she describes as the cannery being the bigger loss.
char/halina_story3	The flat took longer, the way flats do: a letter, then another letter, then a man at the door being very sorry about it.
char/halina_story4	That was thirty years ago and she has not been sad about it for twenty nine of them. This is her street now. She knows every crack in it.
char/halina_mutter1	Thirty years. You would think somebody would have fixed that gate by now.
char/halina_mutter2	That one's got a story he tells three ways. I have heard all three.
say/halina_warm1	You're alright, you. Bit soft, but alright.
say/halina_warm2	Come here. No, come here. There, that's better. Now you look like a person.
say/halina_sharp	I have known you since you had teeth, so do not start with me.
beg/halina_humble	Excuse me love, sorry to bother you. It's just for a bit of bread. You've got a kind face, I can tell.
beg/halina_funny	Two złoty and I will tell you who is sleeping with who on this street. It is a better story than the telly.
beg/halina_blunt	Two złoty. I have been asking on this corner since before you were born and I have got very good at it.
beg/halina_sob	I have got nothing, love. Nothing at all. Thirty years and nothing. Whatever you can spare.
beg/drunk_halina	You. Yes, you. Listen. Listen. No, listen. What was I saying. Two złoty.
cell/lost_halina_1	her good headscarf, the one she keeps for funerals
cell/lost_halina_2	the little tin she keeps her nalewka money in
cell/lost_halina_3	a photograph of the fourth floor flat, taken from the street
police/slipped_halina_1	I have known your mother since school, Marek. Does she know you talk to people like this?
police/slipped_halina_2	Go on, do it. Arrest a sixty one year old woman on a Tuesday. Your sergeant will love that.
char/grazyna_name	Grażyna
char/grazyna_intro	I am not one of these people. I am between flats. My son is a manager in Warsaw and he is going to ring me any day now.
char/grazyna_quirk	Absolutely not a żulica, thank you. Still does her hair every morning with no mirror. Judges everybody on this street while being exactly as skint as all of them. Mentions her son within four minutes of meeting anybody.
char/grazyna_story1	Grażyna had a nice life, and she will tell you about it at length whether you asked or not.
char/grazyna_story2	There was a flat with a balcony, a husband called Janusz who was useless but present, and a son who did his homework.
char/grazyna_story3	Janusz left, the flat went with him somehow, and the son moved to Warsaw and got very busy.
char/grazyna_story4	She has been between flats for four years now. She would like it noted that this is temporary and that she is not like the others.
char/grazyna_mutter1	Look at the state of him. And he thinks he is having a bad day.
char/grazyna_mutter2	My Marek is very busy. That is why. It is not that he does not ring.
say/grazyna_warm1	You are one of the better ones. I am not saying much, mind, but you are.
say/grazyna_warm2	I will say this for you, you keep yourself decent. That matters. Nobody else here thinks so.
say/grazyna_sharp	Do not speak to me like that. I had a balcony.
beg/grazyna_humble	I do not do this. I want you to know I do not do this. But if you had anything at all.
beg/grazyna_funny	Two złoty. Consider it an investment. When my son rings I will have you round for dinner and you will feel very silly about hesitating.
beg/grazyna_blunt	Two złoty, and do not make a performance of it. I have had a very difficult four years.
beg/grazyna_sob	I had a flat with a balcony. A balcony. And now I am stood here asking you. Do you understand what that is like?
beg/drunk_grazyna	I am not drunk, I am tired. There is a difference and it is an important one. Have you got two złoty.
cell/lost_grazyna_1	her hairbrush, which she had for twenty years
cell/lost_grazyna_2	the phone number for her son, written on a bit of card
cell/lost_grazyna_3	her good coat, the one that makes her look like she is going somewhere
police/slipped_grazyna_1	Do you know who my son is? He is a manager. In Warsaw. In an office.
police/slipped_grazyna_2	I am a respectable woman having a difficult period and you will address me properly.
