howto/sec_chars	Making characters and people
howto/chars_1	You can make characters to play as, from the builder under Characters. There are six to begin with, and they are all files you can look at and change.
howto/chars_2	A character is twenty two lines of writing: who they are, what they mutter to themselves when nothing is happening, how they ask for money when they are humble and when they are blunt, and what they find missing when they wake up in a cell.
howto/chars_3	You can write one from nothing, or start from somebody who already exists and only write what you want different. Anything you leave alone falls back to theirs, so somebody can be playable in two minutes and finished properly later.
howto/chars_4	Each question shows you how one of the existing characters answered it, as an example rather than something you are stuck with. Stop whenever you like: it tells you how many lines are still to write and you can come back to it.
howto/chars_5	The people who live in the town are under People, and you can make them as well as change them. Somebody needs seven lines: what they are called, who they are, two things they say, what they say when they are pleased with you, what they say when they have had enough, and what they say when they walk off.
howto/chars_6	Each of them also has a voice, which is what they sound like when they speak, from one for gravelly to five for high. And you can say whether the police are allowed to take them.
howto/chars_7	What they want sits with them too. Everybody loves one thing off the shelf and one thing you can only find, will not touch something, and is merely glad of whole sorts of things. Working that out is most of getting on with people, so no two of them should love the same thing.
howto/chars_8	Somebody can hold a rule instead of a list, which is the more interesting option. A rule is a property, a comparison and a number: how drunk it makes you, more than, nought. That catches every alcoholic drink, and it catches anything alcoholic somebody adds later, which a list never would.
howto/sec_mods	Mods, and sharing what you make
howto/mods_1	A mod is one folder holding everything you have made: your areas, your people, your words and your sounds. You zip it up, send it to somebody, they drop it in their mods folder, and they have it.
howto/mods_2	You do not have to make a mod. The builder works without one, and everything you do goes into the town the game comes with. It warns you once when you start, because an update will put the original back and your work on it will be gone. That is fine for having a poke about; anything you want to keep belongs in a mod.
howto/mods_3	Choose the game you are playing is on the main menu. The town the game comes with is first in the list, and any mod you have sits under it. You pick one, and that is what you are playing and what the builder works on. There is no switch to forget and no wondering which of three things put that bench there.
howto/mods_4	Making one asks what to call it, a sentence describing it if you feel like it, and whether it is extra places added to the town or a town entirely of its own. A town of its own really is its own: only its areas are used, though it still borrows the words and the characters it has not written, so it can never go silent.
howto/mods_5	Each mod keeps its own saved game, so going back to the town the game comes with finds your game exactly where you left it.
howto/mods_6	The first time you pick a mod you have never played, it offers to bring a game across from somewhere else, so forty days of work is not the price of trying somebody's town. Your other game is left exactly as it is. Anything that means nothing in the new town, a person or a drink it does not have, is dropped and you are told what has gone. It only offers once, because after that you have a game there and it will not overwrite it.
howto/mods_7	How the game plays, in the builder, is where the numbers live. What a bottle pays, what washing windscreens gets you, what each job takes out of you, how much a bench brings you round. Change any of them and the mod records only what you changed, so a mod that just makes bottles worth more is a one line file.
howto/mods_8	The same is true of the shop. A mod can put its own drinks and food on the shelves with their own price and strength, change what is already there, and take things off entirely. So somebody can build a town where the only drink is their grandmother's nalewka.
howto/translate_8	Your work is written carefully. It saves to a spare file, reads it back to check every line survived, and only then puts it in place, keeping the old one until the new is safely there.
mod/no_areas	This one has no areas in it yet, so there is nowhere to walk about. Build one in the builder with shift and B, or choose a different game on the main menu.
mod/no_characters	There is nobody to play as. Something has gone wrong with the game's own files, and the repair tool in the options will put them back.
here/which	There are two things here. Which did you mean?
update/failed_partway	The update stopped partway through. %1 of %2 files were replaced before it stopped, so the game is part old and part new. It should still run, but do try again, and if it keeps stopping the repair tool in the options will put everything right. Reason: %3
update/failed_partway	The update stopped partway through. %1 of %2 files were replaced before it stopped, so the game is part old and part new. It should still run, and trying again will pick up where it left off. Would you like me to check everything over first? Reason it stopped: %3
update/check_it_over	Yes, check everything over
update/leave_it	No, leave it
update/checking	Checking every file properly. This takes a moment.
update/all_sound	Everything that was replaced is sound. Try the update again whenever you like.
update/found_wrong	%1 files are not right. Shall I put them back?
update/put_right	Yes, put them right
bld/sounds_yours	Your own sounds, %1 of them. These travel with the mod.
bld/sounds_games	The game's sounds, %1 of them. These do not travel with the mod, so whoever you send it to needs the game.
